# XCU expr
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# expr may be built into the shell but XCU 1.6 also requires it to be
# reachable by exec, so each check runs both ways. Every operator and
# operand is its own argument, and the shell metacharacters among the
# operators are quoted. The locale is POSIX.

assertion  XCU.expr.001
anchor     utilities/expr.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/expr.html#STDOUT
class      required
since      1992
says       + - * / % compute on decimal integers, with the usual
           precedence, left to right for equal precedence, and the result
           is written with a newline; division of non-negative operands is exact integer division.
test       t/001-arithmetic.sh

assertion  XCU.expr.002
anchor     utilities/expr.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       The comparison operators = != < <= > >= give 1 for true and 0
           for false; two integers are compared as numbers and otherwise
           the strings are compared (in the POSIX locale by byte order).
test       t/002-comparison.sh

assertion  XCU.expr.003
anchor     utilities/expr.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       expr1 & expr2 gives expr1 when neither is null or zero and zero
           otherwise; expr1 | expr2 gives expr1 when it is neither null
           nor zero, else expr2 when that is not null, else zero.
test       t/003-and-or.sh

assertion  XCU.expr.004
anchor     utilities/expr.html#EXIT STATUS
class      required
since      1992
says       The exit status is zero when the result is neither null nor
           zero, and one when it is null or zero; the result is still
           written.
test       t/004-exit-status.sh

assertion  XCU.expr.005
anchor     utilities/expr.html#EXIT STATUS
class      required
since      1992
says       An invalid expression (an operator without its operand, or two
           operands with nothing between) exits with status two; division
           by zero exits greater than one.
note       Division by zero is an error but the page does not say which
           of the two error classes it is, so any status of two or more is
           accepted.
test       t/005-invalid.sh

assertion  XCU.expr.006
anchor     utilities/expr.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       expr string : pattern matches the pattern as a basic regular
           expression anchored at the start of the string: it gives the
           number of characters matched, 0 on failure, or the text of the
           first \( \) group when the pattern has one (the null string,
           with status one, when the group did not match).
test       t/006-match.sh

assertion  XCU.expr.007
anchor     utilities/expr.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       Parentheses group, so ( 2 + 3 ) * 4 is 20.
test       t/007-parentheses.sh

assertion  XCU.expr.008
anchor     utilities/expr.html#STDOUT
anchor     utilities/expr.html#EXTENDED DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       A single operand that is a string is written as it is; an
           integer operand may be negative.
test       t/008-single-operand.sh

assertion  XCU.expr.009
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.4
anchor     utilities/expr.html#APPLICATION USAGE
class      required
since      1992
says       expr accepts -- as a first argument and discards it, so that an
           expression starting with a negative integer can be given.
test       t/009-double-dash.sh

# Locale, time zone and message variables (h2a). A locale other than the POSIX locale is made with
# localedef (core/pcloc.sh); making locales is optional (POSIX2_LOCALEDEF), so those tests report
# NOTINUSE without it.

assertion  XCU.expr.010
anchor     utilities/expr.html#ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap08.html#8.2
class      required
says       The string comparison operators of expr use the collating sequence
           of the locale that LC_COLLATE names, with LC_ALL, LANG and a null
           LC_ALL treated as XBD 8.2 says.
timeout    120
test       t/010-locale-collate-compare.sh

assertion  XCU.expr.011
anchor     utilities/expr.html#ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap08.html#8.2
class      required
says       A bracket expression in an expr match can name a collating element
           that the locale named by LC_COLLATE defines (it matches the
           characters of the element together, not singly).
timeout    120
test       t/011-locale-collate-element.sh

assertion  XCU.expr.012
anchor     utilities/expr.html#ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap08.html#8.2
class      required
says       A character class in an expr match is the class of the locale that
           LC_CTYPE names, with LC_ALL, LANG and a null LC_ALL treated as XBD
           8.2 says.
timeout    120
test       t/012-locale-ctype-class.sh
